{"id":"q14","run":1,"role":"calibration","category":"probability","question":"In a Monty Hall variant, the host does not know where the car is and opens one of the two unpicked doors at random; it happens to reveal a goat. Does switching now win with probability 2/3?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"Switching wins with probability 1/2 in this variant, not 2/3.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"In the standard Monty Hall argument, the original pick has a 1/3 chance, so the other closed door holds the remaining 2/3.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The standard 2/3 argument depends on the host always opening a goat door, which makes the host's action informative.","confidence":0.92,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"In this variant the host's random choice carries no information about the car's location.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The observed goat reveal rules out the scenario where the car is behind the opened door, so we must condition on it.","confidence":0.92,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"If the car is behind your door, the path of a goat reveal at door 3 has probability 1/6.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"If the car is behind door 2, the host opening door 3 to show a goat has probability 1/6.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Conditioned on a goat reveal, the car is behind your door with probability 1/2 and behind the other unopened door with probability 1/2.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Switching gains nothing on average, and the 2/3 figure applies only when the host deliberately opens a goat door.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J"